  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 402 AM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nNear term through Thursday...Certainly more than we bargained for\nearly this morning with a slew of warnings issued as there was \njust enough lift upon elevated instability and shear over 40 kts.\nHail as large as 1.5 inches was observed in eastern Marathon \nCounty early this morning. Storms have finally faded in intensity \nas they shifted into far northeast WI (though small hail and heavy\nrain may occur), but some thunder to wake people up early this \nmorning in the Green Bay area and over Door County. Rest of today,\ncannot rule out isolated showers and storms later today into this\nevening, but chances remain pretty low. Risk for severe weather \nlater today has diminished. On Thursday, trend models started to \nshowing yesterday continues, with pops ramping up to 30-45% as \nwave drifts through interacting with daytime heating and some \nbuilding instability. Right entrance region of jet will help \nenhance activity as well. \n\nDry for Friday with high pressure overhead, then moderate to \nheavy rain expected this weekend. Showers and storm chances \n(50-70%) will return to start the weekend as a progressive \nshortwave trough moves across the region. Precipitable water \nvalues increase above the 95th percentile ahead of this system, \nbut seasonably cool temperatures and relatively weak instability \nlook to limit the severe weather risk. Heavy rain risk is there\nthough and WPC has expanded the Slight Risk for excessive rainfall\n(level 2 of 4) over much of our area south of highway 29. Peak of\nrain appears to be Saturday afternoon through Saturday evening.\nLREF probability of 1\" from this event is up to 25%, but looking\nat higher percentiles indicate that the \"ceiling\" with the rainfall\ncould be over 2 inches. Also decent agreement it would affect the\nurban areas of the Fox Valley and northeast WI. This will have to\nbe watched as we get closer. Generally drier and seasonable weather\nis forecast behind the weekend system. \n\nTemperatures/Fog...Temperatures will be seasonably warm into \nThursday with highs in the lower 80s. Slight cooling into the mid \nto upper 70s still pegged for Friday into the weekend. Not a big \ncool down by any stretch. In terms of fog, the overnight storms\nand blowoff clouds from them did hold down the fog, except near\nthe lakeshore and closer to the bay/Door County. More patchy fog\ncould occur tonight, but signal (especially from MOS guidance) is\nnot as strong as it has looked in the last couple nights.",
